.TH BOOT 8
.SH NAME
boot \- how the machine comes up
.SH SYNOPSIS
none
.SH DESCRIPTION
Vectra boots UEFI everywhere, under
.IR limine ,
on three architectures.
On amd64 QEMU presents a
.B q35
machine.
On arm64 and riscv64 QEMU presents its
.B virt
board.
There is no BIOS path and no legacy boot on any of the three.
.SS The boot volume
.IR Build (8)
stages an EFI system partition as a plain host directory,
.BR build/esp ,
which QEMU's
.B vvfat
backend presents to the firmware as a FAT volume.
There is no disk image to make and no loop device to mount.
Under
.BR build/esp/EFI/BOOT
sit the architecture's Limine executable
.RB ( BOOTX64.EFI ,
.B BOOTAA64.EFI
or
.BR BOOTRISCV64.EFI ),
.BR limine.conf ,
and the kernel,
.BR vectra.elf .
Limine looks beside its own executable for its configuration first, so a
.B limine.conf
anywhere else on the volume cannot shadow it.
.PP
.B boot/limine.conf
sets a timeout of zero, so the machine boots straight to the one entry
without a menu, and mirrors Limine's own progress lines to the serial
port.
The entry loads
.B vectra.elf
from the partition the configuration was read from and asks for a
1280x800 screen, which is what
.B q35
gives under UEFI and what the
.B virt
boards' framebuffer device accepts regardless of what is asked.
.PP
Firmware is QEMU's.
On amd64, a combined OVMF image is used when the build finds one beside
the tree.
Otherwise, and on the other two architectures always, the split code
and variable-store images that ship beside the QEMU install are loaded
as two flash devices, the code read-only and the variables copied
somewhere writable first, because UEFI writes to them.
.SS The disks
Two disks are attached over
.B virtio-blk-pci
on every board, the one transport
.IR sd (3)
drives regardless of architecture.
The EFI system partition is
.BR sd0 ,
marked as the boot device.
A second, plain 64 MiB file,
.BR build/disk.img ,
is
.BR sd1 :
a partition table naming a small FAT-typed partition, which the boot
self-test writes a marker into, and a Plan 9 partition from sector 2048
to the end, which
.IR kfs (4)
keeps.
.B sd1
is a real file.
A write to it is still there the next boot.
.B sd0
is QEMU's
.B vvfat
view of
.BR build/esp ,
which reflects a guest write back to a host file as it lands and does
not reliably keep a raw sector write across a boot.
.SS What the kernel does
.I Kmain
brings the machine up in one order, each step reported on a line and
most followed immediately by a self-test of what just came up.
The architecture layer first: the trap tables, before anything that
could fault has anywhere to report to.
Then the screen, the physical memory survey and the heap.
Then the scheduler and the timer.
Then the root device,
.BR #/ ,
attached at
.B /
to build the name space every later process inherits, and the kernel
devices bound into it in turn:
.IP
.EX
#c   /dev    the console, the screen, the keyboard
#s   /srv    posted services
#e   /env    environment variables
#p   /proc   running processes
#b   /bin    the programs the kernel image carries
#l   /lib    the files the kernel image carries
.EE
.PP
The keyboard and mouse controllers come up next, then
.BR #S
at
.BR /dev ,
the disks described above, then the network card if the bus has one.
Ring 3 comes up last of the kernel-only steps, once there is a space to
run a process in and a clock to preempt it with.
.PP
With ring 3 running,
.IR fatfs (4)
starts over
.B sd0
and is mounted at
.BR /n/esp .
.B /n/esp/vectra/bin
is bound before
.B /bin
and
.B /n/esp/vectra/lib
before
.BR /lib ,
so the programs and files the build staged on the disk are what a name
in either now reaches, ahead of the handful the kernel image itself
carries.
Then
.IR kfs (4)
starts over
.BR sd1 's
Plan 9 partition and is mounted at
.BR /usr ,
where
.B $home
is
.BR /usr/glenda .
The last thing the kernel does is run
.BR /bin/rc " " /lib/init
with the console as its three descriptors, described in
.IR init (8).
The boot thread then exits and the machine idles.
.SS The console
Every stage of the boot writes to the serial port, and once the
framebuffer is attached, to the screen as well: the lines from before
that point are replayed so the two agree.
A line from the kernel's own log is tagged in brackets:
.B "[  ok  ]"
for a step or a self-test that passed,
.B "[  \-\-  ]"
for a plain report,
.B "[  !!  ]"
for a step that degraded but did not stop the boot, and
.B "[ FAIL ]"
for a self-test that did not.
A failed self-test names the first check that failed, up to eight of
the later ones as indented
.B also:
lines, and a count of any more beyond that.
An untagged line is not the kernel's log.
It is a program's own output, reached the same way a user's would be:
a line to
.BR /dev/cons ,
or bytes that went out over 9P.
The machine's last tagged line is
.BR "boot complete \-\- idling" ,
after which
.B "boot complete"
is a prompt on the console.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B boot/limine.conf
the bootloader configuration staged into the ESP
.TP
.B build/esp
the staged EFI system partition
.TP
.B build/disk.img
the scratch disk,
.B sd1
.SH SOURCE
.B boot/limine.conf
.br
.B build.odin
.br
.B kernel/main.odin
.br
.B kernel/vfs/root.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR init (8),
.IR build (8),
.IR verify (8),
.IR root (3),
.IR sd (3),
.IR fatfs (4),
.IR kfs (4)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A machine with no FAT partition on
.B sd0
still boots, with only the programs the kernel image carries.
A machine with no
.B /lib/init
logs a warning and idles at
.BR "boot complete" ,
with no shell on any console.
